Date: Sat, 4 Sep 93 23:58:44 WET DST
From: smo@gnu.ai.mit.edu (Shawn O'Connor)
Subject: WAR/NANO/LAW: nanarchy

  Eric Drexler writes, in response to Robin Hanson:

  >If we are considering whether to offer our political support to some
  >growing "movement" in favor of nanarchy, we must consider the possibility
  >that this movement will get out of hand, implementing something that looks
  >to most people like nanarchy, but not to us.

  If technological means emerge for projecting military and police power with
  highly automated systems, then it is likely that they will be used in some
  manner. It seems prudent to formulate a picture of how they might be used
  beneficially, or at least less destructively than when military and police
  power has been subject to discretionary political control.

Why would the political entity choose to obsolete instead of entrench itself?
I would expect such a system to be very much like whatever launches it.  
However, I doubt anything substantially like this will ever be implemented.

I'm not clear on what you mean by 'political entity.'  Do you mean something
like the U.S.?	Something like UCBerkeley?  Why expect it to hold together
throughout the changes?  To simplify the analysis somewhat let's consider
one person in stead of a 'political entity.'

One man makes the breakthrough, he perfects general assemblers.  Within
a few hours he has orders of magnitude more computing power than has ever
existed.  By the end of the day he has uploaded, soon afterwards he is
millenia ahead of the rest of humanity.  Who knows what such an entity
would do, but it could trivially incorporate the planet (humans included).
If events unfold anything like this, I doubt the entity would be concerned 
with whatever humans remain.

  >If technology continues to improve, it's not clear how a nanarchy built on
  >old technology could prevent more advanced attempts at "coercion".  If
  >technological improvement must "run out" before nanarchy, then this seems
  >unlikely before the first intersellar colonization.

  If machine intelligence systems can perform a million years of R&D per
  calendar year (and it seems they can), then it may well be that a good
  understanding of the limits of military technology can be developed rather
  quickly.

If anyone gets millions of years ahead they can do whatever they wish.
Enemies would be insignificant or dead.  Assuming there are any human gnats
left, why would it bother defending them from each other?

  Actually, the ideas are insufficiently detailed, at present, to be
  submitted in detail to anyone. I've spent much of the last ten years trying
  to explain simple molecular machines, to provide a basis for understanding
  just how large the coming jump in technology will be. I sympathize with the
  view that large jumps should be discounted based on historical experience
  and the prevalence of false alarms ("Boys have cried wolf."), but I am
  persuaded that, this time, we face one.

Is there any reason to believe the large jump will be made by only one country?
one group?  one person?  why only one large jump?  I'm not saying that the
pace will be slow, by any means, but one should expect to see a speedup of
distribution and communication channels along with everything else.  There 
are many important advances to come and I don't see why one group will make
them all.  A continuous change throughout the market and its actors is more 
likely than one man or group shooting ahead of the rest.  However, if the
universe will go to whoever first makes working general assemblers, I suppose
the best strategy is to become a molecular nanotech researcher.

  I would encourage Robin to present
  ideas for addressing issues of short-term and long-term military stability
  during and after a rather abrupt transition to a world with molecular
  manufacturing and machine intelligence, without begging the question by
  assuming that the effects of this transition will necessarily be small or
  gradual.

If it's not gradual I don't think we'll be in a position to do anything
about it. 


Shawn
