Date: Sun, 5 Sep 1993 16:49:52 -0400 (EDT)
From: Harry Shapiro <habs@panix.com>
Subject: Tech: X-10 "powerhouse" controls

I am wondering how many, if any list members are using X-10 control
system in their homes or apts??

	X-10 is a control system for lights and household appliances
	which uses electrical wiring to carry the signals. Signals
	are sent from control devices to "relay" modules that are
	placed between the devices and the electrical outlet or
	replace wall switches.

I had always thought they are/were kinda flaky. However, the wiring
design in my current apt. has much to be desired; the location of wall
switches is Very poor. We have two floors and you can't (for example)
when going to the lower level turn off the lights upstairs without
then having to then walk downstairs in the dark.

To solve this "problem" I have started to install a bunch of X-10
equipment. Using an RF based control device, I can now walk through
the house and turn the appropriate lights on and/or off.

Shortly I will solve an other problem. Our downstairs has a single
wall switch controlling two light fixtures (each with two bulbs). I
will use X-10 stuff to enable each bulb to be independently turned on,
off or dimmed.

An other thing I am going to do is place a cheap control device by the
door so that as I leave I can hit a single button and turn every X-10
"light" OFF (preventing me from either getting "yelled" at for leaving
lights on or having to run around in the AM, turning each light off).

I will make an other "report" in a few months, but as far as I can
tell this stuff is great. The Home Automation Lab (HAL) 800 HOMELAB,
has lots of this stuff as does Radio Shack (plug n' power). [FYI --
HAL has a fancy system that runs under MS-Windows that allows all
devices to be completely programmed, add voice control, etc.]

